import Native

{-
   `Size`, `USize` and `CodeUnit` - the three integers whose width the *target* chooses rather than
   the language. Analysis-Modules.md Move 2, and doc/spec/types.md's target-width family.

   The point of this fixture is that **the same source compiles and answers the same number on both
   targets while the widths differ**: a `Size` is sixty-four bits natively and thirty-two on
   JavaScript, and a `CodeUnit` is eight bits against sixteen. Nothing below names a width, and
   nothing below is `@platform`-split.

   What it holds the conversion ladder to is the rule that makes the bound two-ended - see
   `integerRangeFits`. A conversion *into* a target-width type is judged against the smallest it may
   be, and a conversion *out of* one against the largest, so:

     - `Int` widens into `Size` and `U32` does not, because a `U32` does not fit a signed word of
       thirty-two bits;
     - `Size` widens into `I64` and not into `Int`;
     - `Size` and `USize` are the same word, so `bitcast` relates them - and relates neither to any
       fixed width, because a reinterpretation needs two widths to compare and these have none.

   The *address* rung - `Bitcast(%a, Size)`, which is the other pair and the reason the integer side
   moved off `I64` - is not exercised here, because there is nothing portable to assert about the
   number it produces. `Native/Pointer.yana` is where it is used and `resolve/Heap.yana` is where a
   fixture already reads one.

   The negative half of that lives in Reject.Bits.yana and in the ladder itself; what is here is
   every direction that must keep working.
-}

@noinline fn opaque(x: Size) -> Size = x

-- The widening every subscript depends on: an ordinary `Int` index reaches a `Size` position with
-- no conversion written. This is the one that decides whether `xs[i]` compiles at all.
fn indexed(xs: [Int], i: Int) -> Int = xs[i]

-- And out the other side, which is the direction the bound's high end decides.
fn asLong(n: Size) -> I64 = n
fn asWord(n: Int) -> Size = n

-- The unsigned reading, which is what a bounds test compares at: read as an unsigned number of the
-- same width, a negative index is above every length there is.
fn unsignedAbove(a: Size, b: Size) -> Bool = (bitcast(a) :: USize) > (bitcast(b) :: USize)

-- A code unit is an unsigned integer of the target's encoding width, and widens into `Int` on both
-- targets because eight bits and sixteen both fit one.
fn unitValue(u: CodeUnit) -> Int = u

fn main() -> Int:
    let &score = 0

    let xs = [10, 20, 30]
    if indexed(xs, 2) == 30 then score = score + 1

    if asLong(opaque(7)) == 7 then score = score + 2
    if asWord(7) == 7 then score = score + 4

    -- Negative read unsigned is above every non-negative value, at whatever width the target picked.
    if unsignedAbove(opaque(0 - 1), opaque(1000000)) then score = score + 8
    if !unsignedAbove(opaque(1), opaque(2)) then score = score + 16

    -- `truncate` back down, which is the only spelling that compiles on both targets: the conversion
    -- is a real narrowing natively and the identity on JS, and the ladder has a rung for each.
    if (truncate(opaque(300)) :: Int) == 300 then score = score + 32

    -- A code unit, through the one operation that is the same on both encodings: an ASCII value.
    if unitValue(65) == 65 then score = score + 64

    return score
